Before: every new shape means editing this method.

```java
double area(Shape shape) {
    switch (shape.type) {
        case CIRCLE:    return Math.PI * shape.radius * shape.radius;
        case RECTANGLE: return shape.width * shape.height;
        // adding TRIANGLE means editing this switch
    }
}
```

After: each shape owns its own area calculation; `area()` never changes again.

```java
interface Shape {
    double area();
}
class Circle implements Shape {
    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}
class Triangle implements Shape {
    public double area() { return 0.5 * base * height; }
}
```

This is the strategy pattern in miniature: behavior that varies by type gets pushed into implementations of a shared interface, instead of a branch that has to know every type.
